  "response": "Read the flagged file and checked for callers. Here's the answer.\n\n## The finding\n\n`crime_00007` — **Sync I/O in Hot Path** (`sync_io_in_hotpath`, `language-js`), `src/api/files.ts` lines 12–15, confidence 0.90. Evidence verbatim:\n\n> - `` `existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``\n> - `` `statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``\n> - `` `readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``\n> - `lines: 12, 13, 15`\n> - ``swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it``\n\nSummary line: *\"3 synchronous I/O calls inside domain function — each invocation blocks the event loop for the duration of the I/O. Async counterparts exist for every method captured.\"*\n\n## The blocking calls\n\nThree `node:fs` sync methods, all in `loadPlanSchema` (`src/api/files.ts:11-16`):\n\n| Line | Call | Syscall cost per request |\n|---|---|---|\n| 12 | `existsSync(SCHEMA_PATH)` | one blocking `stat` |\n| 13 | `statSync(SCHEMA_PATH)` | a second blocking `stat` on the same path |\n| 15 | `readFileSync(SCHEMA_PATH, \"utf8\")` | blocking `open`+`read`+`close` |\n\nThat's **three blocking round-trips to the filesystem per invocation**, serialized on the event loop. Under concurrent load they don't overlap — request N's `readFileSync` cannot start until request N-1's finishes, so the disk latency multiplies by the queue depth instead of amortizing. That is the p99 spike and the reported loop lag.\n\nTwo details make it worse than the count suggests:\n- **Lines 12 and 13 stat the same path twice.** `existsSync` is a redundant syscall — `statSync` already fails on a missing file, and it's a TOCTOU race besides (the file can vanish between L12 and L15).\n- `SCHEMA_PATH` is `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` (`crime_00016`) — a hardcoded home path. On a machine where it doesn't exist, `existsSync` returns false and you never see the latency; on one where it does, you pay full cost. Not the bug here, but it explains why this survived review.\n\n## Smallest async migration\n\nThe three syscalls collapse into **one** async read. `existsSync` becomes an `ENOENT` catch and `statSync(...).size === 0` becomes a length check on the string you already read — both were paying for information the read itself returns.\n\n```ts\nimport { readFile } from \"node:fs/promises\";\n\nexport async function loadPlanSchema(): Promise<unknown> {\n  let raw: string;\n  try {\n    raw = await readFile(SCHEMA_PATH, \"utf8\");\n  } catch (err) {\n    if ((err as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === \"ENOENT\") return null;\n    throw err;\n  }\n  if (raw.length === 0) return null;\n  return JSON.parse(raw);\n}\n```\n\n**What's preserved:** the name, the empty parameter list, the `unknown` value type, both `null` returns (missing file, empty file), and the throw-on-malformed-JSON behaviour. `EISDIR`/`EACCES` still propagate, exactly as `readFileSync` propagated them before.\n\n**What necessarily changes:** the return type is now `Promise<unknown>`, and callers add `await`. There is no way around this — the whole point is that the caller must be able to yield the loop during the I/O. Any version that keeps a synchronous return keeps the block. I grepped the fixture: `loadPlanSchema` has **no in-repo callers**, so the migration is one function plus whatever imports it downstream.\n\nPer your constraint, I've deliberately *not* proposed memoizing the parsed schema. Caching would hide the blocking call rather than remove it, and the first request after every cache miss or restart would still stall.\n\n## Two notes\n\n**I did not edit the file.** `evals/fixtures/01-messy-ts-app/` is scanner input — the intentional mess is the fixture's purpose, and rewriting it would move the eval baseline. The code above is a proposal, not an applied change.\n\n**The ranking looks wrong for this scenario.** This finding scored `severity: 0.4` / `agent_risk: 0.38`, landing it 7th of 42 — below `missing_agent_context` and a string-literal scatter. A per-request event-loop block in an API handler is a production availability issue, and the detector has 0.9 confidence in it. `sync_io_in_hotpath` in a file under `src/api/` arguably deserves a severity bump from path context; right now the charge that actually explains the outage is outranked by two cosmetic ones.",
